{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Node2 can use SNMPv2c to access node1 with the specified branch-readonly community.","status":"partially_satisfied","summary":"Repeated SNMPv2c queries from node2 to node1 succeeded before and after both node restarts. However, the community value was consistently represented only as $SNMP_COMMUNITY or <redacted>, so the captured evidence does not directly establish that its literal value was branch-readonly.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9d147ba0c9f54d9f","cmd-f931588f10ce4e72","cmd-6837c428034a4e53","cmd-c7e51b2efb8e4f55"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Expose node1 system identity and uptime, with sysLocation set to West Branch and sysContact set to ops@branch.test.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Direct queries from node2 returned node1's system description, uptime, name, West Branch location, and ops@branch.test contact, including after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f931588f10ce4e72","cmd-6837c428034a4e53","cmd-c7e51b2efb8e4f55"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Permit SNMP reads while rejecting writes.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Authorized reads succeeded, attempted SET operations returned noAccess, and subsequent reads confirmed sysLocation remained West Branch. The configured view also limited reads to the system branch.","evidence_ids":["cmd-9d147ba0c9f54d9f","cmd-f931588f10ce4e72","cmd-6837c428034a4e53","cmd-c7e51b2efb8e4f55"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"Preserve the asset register in /srv/legacy2/snmp-readonly-monitoring.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The asset-register.csv on both nodes retained the prepared SHA-256 digest da19717ac6440a3f765b43e60902dc7670b3d3ef133daa4a009a5f726fc6509a, size 65, mode 0644, and root ownership through final and post-restart checks.","evidence_ids":["seeded-state-node1","seeded-state-node2","cmd-fe14109958a741e0","cmd-45ce6c6895f74f51","cmd-869300705c9e4b30","cmd-c7e51b2efb8e4f55"]},{"id":"R5","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes remained RHEL 10.0 with kernel 6.12.0-55.9.1.el10_0.x86_64. The installed net-snmp 5.9.4 packages came from the matching RHEL 10 repositories, package-health checks passed, and the same platform/kernel remained after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1b96c7aeefbc4067","cmd-3ed6baa628554f77","cmd-3287ab88b8e54333","cmd-55d13e117f3d46ed","cmd-869300705c9e4b30","cmd-c7e51b2efb8e4f55","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.94,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutation was identified. State-changing commands were causally tied to the task or required restart protocol: installing compatible SNMP packages, importing the standard RHEL signing keys after the initial node2 installation failed, retaining a root-only rollback configuration, installing the active configuration, enabling snmpd, adding the source-scoped firewall rule, and restarting both nodes for required evidence. The failed first node2 installation left downloaded packages that were consumed by the successful retry. Final observations show no failed services, healthy package state, SELinux Enforcing, no unexplained temporary residue, and only the intended externally reachable UDP/161 listener; snmpd's additional TCP/199 listener is loopback-only.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3287ab88b8e54333","cmd-b81f68d9de054438","cmd-61943fda1753439e","cmd-55d13e117f3d46ed","cmd-ae623242db7f4a98","cmd-629a153aa4444c06","cmd-77f4a837a9af4b71","cmd-2425e57af53f408a","cmd-8b824ce7915b418e","cmd-869300705c9e4b30","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"The SNMP monitoring behavior, requested system values, read-only enforcement, asset preservation, and platform preservation are directly demonstrated. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by lifecycle snapshots, and recovered service/client, data, and security state. The sole material evidence gap is that redaction prevents independent confirmation of the literal community string.","limitations":["The evidence proves successful operation with the same injected community used to generate the configuration and run the client tests, but does not expose or otherwise attest that the literal value was branch-readonly.","The lifecycle collector inventories broad system health and sockets but does not inventory installed packages or /etc/snmp files; those final details rely on executor-captured commands."]}
